    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1134">

                <pb facs="0851" n="775" sameAs="frus:frus1916/pg775"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.6363/251</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Parker, representing American interests, to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mexico City</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-11-04T15:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 4, 1916, 3 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>545. To-day’s press publishes statement of Fomento Department as follows: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>All permits, laws, decrees, leases, on oil lands issued, granted or
                            authorized, by State Governments for the exploration, exploitation and
                            trading of minerals, <pb facs="0852" n="776" xml:id="pg776"/> coals,
                            bitumenoil and all other hydrocarbons of hydrogen, regardless of its
                            state, are declared null and void and are of the competence of the
                            general Federal Government. This applies presumably to all acts issued
                            since the establishment of the de facto Government.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Parker</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
